t $g(s)=f(\\frac{L}{\\pi }s),$ we get the Fourier series of $f(x):$\n\n$$\nf(x)∼\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi }{L}x)+{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi }{L}x)\n$$\n\nTherefore, the constant term of $f(x)$ is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{0} & =\\frac{1}{\\pi }{\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }g(s)\\,\\text{d}s \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\pi }{\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }f(\\frac{L}{\\pi }s)\\,\\text{d}s \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\pi }{\\int }_{-L}^{L}f(\\frac{L}{\\pi }\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{L}x)\\,\\text{d}(\\frac{\\pi }{L}x) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\pi }\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{L}{\\int }_{-L}^{L}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{L}{\\int }_{-L}^{L}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting $L=4$ and $f(x)={cos}^{2}⁡(x),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{0} & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot {\\int }_{-4}^{4}{cos}^{2}⁡(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186423256 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186423251 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186423256 #edb/ref 1069 "q-341817" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186423256 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186423256 #edb/ref 1071 "$$\nf(x)∼\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx)+{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx)\n$$\n\nThe Fourier series of the $2\\pi $-periodic extension $f(x)$ of the function \n\n$$\nF(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}-2, & x\\in [0,\\pi ) \\\\ 3, & x\\in [-\\pi ,0)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nis shown above. Find the exact value of ${a}_{4}.$\n\n${a}_{4}=$ {{field-1}}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186423256 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "fc8861f3-8c91-59c6-b70f-78e5364379e8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186423256 #edb/ref 1074 "For the Fourier series of a $2L$-periodic function, the cosine coefficients are given by\n\n$$\n{a}_{n}=\\frac{1}{L}{\\int }_{-L}^{L}f(x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi }{L}x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nIn our case, $L=\\pi .$ Also, $f(x)=-2$ for $x\\in [0,\\pi )$ and $f(x)=3$ for $x\\in [-\\pi ,0).$ So, substituting into the formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{4} & =\\frac{1}{\\pi }{\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }f(x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\pi }{\\int }_{-\\pi }^{0}3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x)\\,\\text{d}x+\\frac{1}{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }(-2)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{\\pi }\\cdot \\frac{1}{4}{[\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x)]}_{-\\pi }^{0}-\\frac{2}{\\pi }\\cdot \\frac{1}{4}{[\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x)]}_{0}^{\\pi } \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{4\\pi }(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0)-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(-4\\pi ))-\\frac{1}{2\\pi }(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4\\pi )-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0)) \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
